formance in 1938 when he  played a whole season with ALS is  perhaps the most remarkable  single-season accomplishment in baseball  history but Jackie Robinson playing  every day in 1947 knowing the burden  that is on him knowing that half of the  guys out there on the other teams are  opposed to him playing and in many cases  are taunting him sometimes trying to  hurt him it's certainly right up there  and and what he does his ability to  recognize that without the hitting  nothing else matters if he doesn't  continue to play well it's not going to  happen for him all season long he  continues to hit he doesn't skip a day  doesn't take a day off you know when the  season begins it's not clear that he's  even going to be a regular first baseman  the Dodgers are carrying two of their  first baseman on the roster but over  time Branch Rickey who's trying to show  Jackie Robinson to support starts  peeling them off he traded them away he  trades away some of the opponents of  integration as well he trades kirby  higbe e to the Pirates because Higby had  been so outspoken in his belief that  blacks should not be in baseball he  keeps Dixie Walker though because he  needs him frankly and and Branch Rickey  still wants to win so going down the  stretch into the end of the season  the Dodgers go to st. Louis for on their  last road trip of the year with a chance  to clinch the pennant and the Cardinals  have been one of the worst antagonists  of Robinson all year early in the season  at least a handful of players threatened  to boycott their game it's not clear  whether it really amounted to a  full-fledged  threat in which the entire team was  talking about walking but at least a few  of them were threatened to walk back in  August  eNOS slaughter  and Spike Jackie Robinson on a close  play at first base that Robinson  believed was intentional and actually  most of the people in the press corps  even in the white press corps said it  looked like it was intentional and if 