t  year as a freshman and was shocked to  see that Stanford was all white because  his baseball team his favorite team was  was integrated why shouldn't Stanford be  integrated and he started the first  chapter of the n-double a-c-p at  Stanford and got to school segregated  school integrated excuse me and then  spent the rest of his career working as  a fundraiser for the end of  KCT these are the ripples that jackie  robinson is causing in 1947 when now to  take you back to st. Louis and that that  final series against the Cardinals I  want you know to see what what we what  how it felt in 1947 at the end of that  season in this game Robinson has spiked  yet again this is the last game of the  year on the road against the Cardinals  their dreaded rivals and Joe Garagiola a  close play at first drives his spikes  into Jackie Robinson's foot so even now  after he's approved himself he's already  been named the Rookie of the Year for  the league they used to give you a word  then before the end of the seasons  Robinson is still facing these threats  he still feels like Garagiola may have  done it on purpose Garagiola has always  said he didn't but next time up when  Robinson comes up to bat he says  something over his shoulder to Garagiola  he feels like he's been around long  enough now that maybe he can give a  little bit of lip he's established  himself well enough that he can let a  little bit of that anger out and  Garagiola it was also a bit of a hothead  gets up from the Crouch and he and  Jackie Robinson are chest to chest ready  to go at it until they're pulled apart  by the umpire and by the coaches who  rush out of the dugout next time he  comes up to bat Jackie Robinson hits a  home run it gives the Dodgers a two to  one lead going into the ninth inning and  Robinson was playing first base he  played all play first base in 1947 not  second there's a foul ball with two outs  hit over by the Dodger dugout and  Robinson goes over to try to catch it  leans over and the dugout catches the  ball it starts to fall in and and Ralph  Branca his teammate leaps from his seat  and grabs Robinson and pushes him back  onto the field and tackles him on the  grass soft landing what otherwise might  have been very painful and the team all  rushes out of the dugout and they  congratulate Robinson on this great  catch they congratulate Ralph Branca on  the other great catch and Robinson says  after the game that he was so pleased  because a few months earlier he didn't  know that anybody on the team would have  bothered to break his fall so the team  at that point is celebrating that  they've locked up the pennant they get  on the train they go back to Brooklyn  and play dough back to New York they  pull into Penn Station and waiting for  them there are thousands and thousands  of fans  to greet them to congratulate them with  signs saying bring on the Yankees we're  the champs we you know Jackie Robinson  gets off this train and he rushes to a  phone booth to try to call his wife to  say he's back and this mob starts  following him and they trap him in the  phone booth it's probably the first time  in American history that a black man has  been chased by a white mob intent on  hugging him he escapes from the from the  phone booth and with a police escort and  makes his way for the subway again these  ballplayers did not have limousines  waiting for them back then and these  fans are all following him they want to  pay his subway fare for him and they  crowd into the train with him and just  escort him home the train car is packed  with Dodger fans I looked through that  picture to the pictures of that event  The Daily News there were lots of photos  of the celebration looking for images of  my father or my grandfather thinking  that it wouldn't it be nice if they'd  been there that day and I think just  like these guys today who all want to be  remembered as part of the Jackie  Robinson story who all want to be  connected with this great man even  though they wouldn't shake his hand at  the start of the season it's an  association we all want to have today  and I thought it was wonderful to see so  many ballplayers on Sunday wearing the  number 42  some people said to me do you think it  was overkill do you think it was  commercialism I said no I think this is  a guy that nobody wanted to touch he had  to stay in separate hotels from his team  he had to eat on his own he couldn't  travel in certain cities without being  you know afraid for violence there were  death threats and now 60 years later  everyone wants to wear his number I  think it's great and I think it's  because he stands for something so much  bigger than baseball democracy was put  to the test after the war Jackie  Robinson was the leading symbol of the  new way of doing things he proved that  if you gave some people a chance if you  give Outsiders new ideas if you give  these things a chance  it makes your team better it made the  Dodgers better it made America better  and I'd like to think that by writing  this book I've helped to set the story  straight I've helped to return Robinson  to his full humanity I'd rather see him  as a real three-dimensional  then an eight-foot statue in bronze and  I've found my way of connecting to the  story as well and I'm proud to be able  to tell the story and I'm happy to be  able to share it with you today so I  thank you for coming and we have some  time for questions and I would just ask  that you come down he's on the  microphone so that the cameras will be  able to pick up your your questions yes  awesome into Dubai  so I was just curious what was there did  you do in your research when your  research did you did you detect that  kind of schizophrenic perception on the  part of black America to accept baseball  I mean to accept Jackie Robinson's  introduction into white baseball but yet  realizing that it would mean the demise  of the black baseball league and the  other question I have