o go but they're  gonna be a whole lot of us left behind  and even in 1947 attendance was way down  at Negro league ballparks so they could  see it's not as if this was just the  first whiff of the of a foul odor this  they knew right away that there would  not be that they would not last long  Jackie Robinson had it had a huge impact  right away their question on the side  one of the reactions was by Langston  Hughes in a famous passage about when  beginning when I saw Jackie Robinson hit  that home run my question is was Jackie  Robinson aware of Hughes reaction and  did he personally know Langston Hughes  that's a good question I don't know that  there was any contact between Robinson  and Hughes there was also a great Count  Basie song and that was the first lyrics  were written in 1947 did you see Jackie  Robinson hit that ball Hughes was  writing columns all season long in the  Chicago Defender and about Jackie  Robinson so he was writing about him  often I'm sure he must have seen him  play but there's no I'd no evidence that  there was contact Jackie Robinson's main  the press was Wendell Smith of the  Pittsburgh courier Wendell Smith was his  roommate on the road the Dodgers  actually hired him to travel with  Robinson because none of about white  ballplayers would room with him so and  in cities where they couldn't find  hotels with where the white and black  players couldn't stay together Wendell  Smith and Jackie Robinson would go off  on their own and find either houses to  stay with you know prominent black  citizens homes or in black owned hotels  and it was up to Wendell Smith often to  arrange for black taxi companies to come  pick them up and find like restaurants  where they could eat so Wendell Smith  was was a very important player in this  in the story of Jackie Robinson's  success in 1947 yes yes I was curious  about Jackie Robinson's salary in 1947  compared to other rookies with his same  skill sets and also not only 47 but in  subsequent years of his career in 1947  Jackie Robinson made the league minimum  $5,000 and he probably brought in at  least one hundred two hundred thousand  dollars in extra revenues for the  Dodgers that year just because they were  attracting so many fans everywhere they  went and he certainly made Branch Rickey  a wealthy man and the following season  Branch Rickey rewarded him with a very  small raise and Jackie Robinson accepted  the first offer that Rickey gave him in  1948 so he was making in 48 far less  than other players on the Dodgers who  were not nearly as talented but perhaps  were older and as a result had built up  Robin Ricky was a even no matter how  enlightened he was another way as he was  not very enlightened when it came to to  writing checks and he really believed  that Robinson would not was not entitled  to big raise until he proved himself a  few more years in the league and  Robinson always I think felt great  admiration for Rickey but his wife in  particular was was bitter about that  that issue yes  you said that you started out the book  with the story about peewee Reese and  Jackie Robinson and did the research and  found that it the the actual events did  not happen the way sort of the myth was  created and I remember the children's  book that you had mentioned there was a  short children's book about that did you  were able to sort of to find out how  this myths began yeah I think the myth  began because peewee Reese and Jackie  Robinson did become very good friends in  later years and forty-eight Jackie  Robinson switches from first base to  second so their DoublePlay partners and  they're often together on the field  during pitching changes during timeouts  and I think people got used to seeing  them together and I'll you know how it  is on a pitching change the guys will  get together in the middle infield and  tell jokes and rib each other and that  was a startling sight and I think in 48  and 49 Robinson was still getting some  some heckling probably throughout his  career he was still getting heckling  from racists and I do think at some  point Rob Rickey excuse me Pee Wee made  a point of going over and putting an arm  around Robinson's shoulder to silence  those hecklers because Robinson and  Rickey both said they remembered an  incident Robinson said it was in 48 in  Boston I think and Reese said it was in  Philadelphia in 49 neither of them put  it in Cincinnati in 47 and I just think  that writers over the years felt like  the story would be a little bit more  powerful a little bit more evocative if  you made it in that first season 1947  not only is that unfortunate because  accuracy is to be cherished but it's  also unfortunate because it sends the  message that Robinson couldn't have done  it without the help of this white man  and I think that diminishes his  accomplishment because he was very much  alone that year and nobody on the team  ever invited him out to dinner  in 1947 nobody on the team asked his  wife to sit with the other wives or to  visit with them when the guys were on  the road these guys had to prove  themselves and had to establish  themselves before anybody was really  willing to give him a chance and that's  not as easy to capture in a statue but  that's the truth of the matter yes  another question on this side yes thank  you  first of all I enjoyed your speech very  much your talk very much  I've heard that Philadelphia was very  rough for him and I'm just wondering in  the different cities was the extent of  the racism and the hatred and the  threats the reflection of the geographic  makeup of the teammates or the manager  or the ownership of the team were just  the city in general and a second  question is what can you briefly say  about Larry Doby and what he went  through on the kind of person he was I  think that it was particularly each city  to various environments I mean in  Philadelphia I think it was largely the  result of Ben Chapman the manager of the  Phil